Yup, just Murphy's Law.

See the food-343 spoiler...  When you eat a corpse (fresh or tinned)
that can give one of the "standard" intrinsics (poison/sleep/fire/
cold/disintegration/shock resistance, teleportitis, teleport control,
or telepathy), an intrinsic is randomly selected from those the
monster can confer without considering intrinsics you may already
have.

Then, with probability LEV/CHANCE based on the monster's base level
(LEV) and an intrinsic-dependent factor (CHANCE), you get the
intrinsic if you don't already have it, again independent of what
other intrinsics you may already have.

A tengu with base level LEV=6 can confer three intrinsics---poison,
teleportitis, and teleport control with CHANCE=15, 10, and 12
respectively.  If you do the calculation, you find that you'll get:
poison resistance (13%), teleportitis (20%), teleport control (17%),
or nothing (50%).

Nymphs and leprechauns are lower-level (3 and 5 respectively), but
since they can *only* confer teleportitis, the probability of getting
that intrinsic ends up being higher: 30% from a nymph, 50% from a
leprechaun.  And these are independent of whether or not you already
have teleport control.

